    <header>
        <h1>The Top 10 Video Games of All Time</h1>
        <p>Architecting a definitive look at the titles that shaped gaming history.</p>
    </header>

    <main>
        <h2>The Definitive Rankings</h2>
        <p>The following list represents the pinnacle of game design, storytelling, and cultural impact. These titles did not just entertain; they <em>redefined</em> what the medium could achieve.</p>

        <ol>
            <li>
                <h3>The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time</h3>
                <p>This masterpiece successfully transitioned the action-adventure genre into 3D space. It established the <strong>Z-targeting system</strong> and camera mechanics that modern games still rely on today, making it an <em>undeniable</em> milestone in industry history.</p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <h3>Tetris</h3>
                <p>Simple to learn yet impossible to truly master, this puzzle classic transcends generations. Its geometric perfection represents the <strong>purest form of gameplay mechanics</strong> ever created, ensuring its timeless appeal.</p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <h3>Grand Theft Auto V</h3>
                <p>Setting a benchmark for open-world design, this title combined three parallel narratives with an incredibly detailed environment. It stands as a <strong>monumental commercial achievement</strong> that continues to influence modern multiplayer ecosystems.</p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <h3>Minecraft</h3>
                <p>By transforming players from consumers into creators, this sandbox phenomenon captured global imagination. Its infinite procedural worlds offer a level of <strong>unprecedented creative freedom</strong> that changed indie development forever.</p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <h3>Super Mario Bros.</h3>
                <p>This side-scrolling triumph virtually <strong>saved the home console industry</strong> after the 1983 crash. Its precise physics and brilliant level design made Mario a permanent global icon.</p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <h3>The Witcher 3: Wild Hunt</h3>
                <p>This title raised the bar for dark fantasy storytelling and mature narrative choices. Every side quest feels like a <strong>cohesive short story</strong>, proving that open-world games can deliver incredibly deep plots without sacrificing scale.</p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <h3>Portal 2</h3>
                <p>A flawless puzzle-platformer that balances genius mechanics with exceptional humor. The relationship between the player and the rogue AI components creates an <em>exceedingly rare</em> perfect narrative pacing.</p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <h3>Dark Souls</h3>
                <p>By rejecting modern hand-holding trends, this dark fantasy epic birthed an entirely new genre. Its intricate, interconnected world design and emphasis on <strong>patience and environmental storytelling</strong> forced the industry to rethink game difficulty.</p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <h3>Half-Life 2</h3>
                <p>This first-person shooter revolutionized narrative delivery by never taking control away from the player during cutscenes. Its integration of <strong>physics-based puzzles</strong> into regular combat remains a masterclass in immersive design.</p>
            </li>

            <li>
                <h3>Doom (1993)</h3>
                <p>The foundational pioneer of the first-person shooter genre. Its fast-paced action, shareware distribution model, and <strong>revolutionary 3D engine graphics</strong> laid the groundwork for modern PC gaming and online multiplayer culture.</p>
            </li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Honorable Mentions</h2>
        <p>While they missed the top ten, games like <em>Chrono Trigger</em> and <em>Resident Evil 4</em> deserve immense credit for their genre-defining innovations.</p>
    </main>

